From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jim Martin <jimmartin@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 20:55:55 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] thunderbolt: qcom: Start the USB host again when the port
 returns to native USB

The SuperSpeed port of the USB controller that a host router tunnels has
one PIPE interface. A TCSR bit routes it to the router or to the native
USB3 side of the PHY. When a partner leaves USB4 but stays attached, for
example an NVMe enclosure that was given no PCIe tunnel and falls back to
USB 3, the router is stopped: the PHY is initialised again for the native
mode and the route is switched back, under a running xHCI.

The xHCI's SuperSpeed port can come out of that unable to detect a
receiver. Its PORTSC stays in RxDetect while the partner keeps waiting on
SuperSpeed, the PHY's registers read as they do on an idle port, and
neither switching the port's power off and on nor a runtime suspend and
resume of the controller brings the port back. A reset of the host
controller does, within about 100 ms. On a Surface Laptop 8 with the
controller kept awake, as it is before udev allows it to runtime suspend,
the enclosure stayed missing after six of seven hand-backs, three of them
at boot.

The platform's Windows drivers do not switch the interface under a
running host: they start the host afresh on every plug, once the PHY is
set up, and start USB4 only after that.

Take the host away before the hand-back and let it start again
afterwards, through the controller's USB role switch, which is how the
host comes and goes with a plug. The router does not decide the role.
It holds the switch in the device role, asked for by name since a
controller may take "none" to mean host, and releases it after the
hand-back. The switch then takes the role the Type-C driver asked for
last: the host again for a partner that fell back to plain USB, and no
host for a partner that was unplugged or took the host role itself
meanwhile. While the switch is held the Type-C driver cannot start a
host that would run across the hand-back.

The switch is held whatever role the controller is in at the time. The
controller changes its role from a work item, so one that reports no
host may be about to start the host it was asked for a moment ago. The
hold returns once the controller has left the host role, so there is no
wait with a time limit after which the hand-back would go ahead under a
running host. A controller that has no host, as after an unplug, is not
asked to do anything.

A router stopped for system sleep is left alone: the partner has not
left USB4 then and the router is started again at resume.

Assisted-by: Claude Fable 5.2
Signed-off-by: Jim Martin <jimmartin@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/thunderbolt/Kconfig          |  1 +
 drivers/thunderbolt/qcom-usb4-host.c | 78 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 79 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/thunderbolt/Kconfig b/drivers/thunderbolt/Kconfig
index 13e1f7d57..62c43c62d 100644
--- a/drivers/thunderbolt/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/thunderbolt/Kconfig
@@ -94,6 +94,7 @@ config USB4_QCOM
 	depends on CLK_GLYMUR_GCC
 	depends on DRM_MSM
 	depends on TYPEC
+	depends on USB_ROLE_SWITCH || !USB_ROLE_SWITCH
 	depends on RESET_CONTROLLER
 	select DRM_DISPLAY_HELPER
 	help
diff --git a/drivers/thunderbolt/qcom-usb4-host.c b/drivers/thunderbolt/qcom-usb4-host.c
index cf331221b..a9ecf8438 100644
--- a/drivers/thunderbolt/qcom-usb4-host.c
+++ b/drivers/thunderbolt/qcom-usb4-host.c
@@ -39,6 +39,7 @@
 #include <linux/soc/qcom/glymur-usb4.h>
 #include <linux/soc/qcom/smem.h>
 #include <linux/soc/qcom/socinfo.h>
+#include <linux/usb/role.h>
 #include <linux/usb/typec.h>
 #include <linux/usb/typec_mux.h>
 #include <linux/usb/typec_tbt.h>
@@ -1144,12 +1145,89 @@ static void qcom_usb4_host_stop_all(struct qcom_usb4_host *host)
 	qcom_usb4_host_power_off(host);
 }
 
+/*
+ * The SuperSpeed port of the controller named by "usb4-host-interface" has
+ * one PIPE interface, which a TCSR bit routes either to the router, for the
+ * USB3 tunnel, or to the PHY's native USB3 side. Stopping the router
+ * initialises the PHY again for the native mode and switches the route back.
+ * An xHCI that runs across that can be left with a SuperSpeed port that
+ * detects no receiver until the host controller is reset, so a partner that
+ * fell back from USB4 to plain USB never appears. The firmware's own drivers
+ * never switch the interface under a running host either: they start the host
+ * afresh on every plug, once the PHY is set up, and USB4 only after that.
+ *
+ * So take the host away before the hand-back and let it start again
+ * afterwards, through the controller's role switch, which is how the host
+ * comes and goes with a plug. The router does not decide the role: the switch
+ * is held in the device role for the hand-back, and takes the role the Type-C
+ * driver asked for last when it is released. That is the host again for a
+ * partner that fell back to plain USB, and no host for one that was unplugged
+ * or took the host role itself meanwhile.
+ *
+ * The switch is held whatever its role is at the time: the controller changes
+ * its role from a work item, so a host that was asked for a moment ago may not
+ * be there yet. The hold returns once the controller has done what it was
+ * asked, and no host is started until the release.
+ */
+static struct usb_role_switch *qcom_usb4_host_usb_host_leave(struct qcom_usb4_host *host)
+{
+	struct usb_role_switch *sw;
+	int ret;
+
+	if (!host->tunnel_ctrl)
+		return NULL;
+	sw = usb_role_switch_find_by_fwnode(dev_fwnode(host->tunnel_ctrl));
+	if (!sw) {
+		dev_warn_once(host->dev, "%s has no USB role switch, its host stays across a hand-back\n",
+			      dev_name(host->tunnel_ctrl));
+		return NULL;
+	}
+	/* Not "none": a controller may be told to be a host by default. */
+	ret = usb_role_switch_hold(sw, USB_ROLE_DEVICE);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_warn(host->dev, "%s: USB role not held for the hand-back: %d\n",
+			 dev_name(host->tunnel_ctrl), ret);
+		usb_role_switch_put(sw);
+		return NULL;
+	}
+	/* Held, but the controller did not do as it was asked */
+	if (usb_role_switch_get_role(sw) == USB_ROLE_HOST)
+		dev_warn(host->dev, "%s kept its USB host into the hand-back\n",
+			 dev_name(host->tunnel_ctrl));
+	return sw;
+}
+
+static void qcom_usb4_host_usb_host_return(struct qcom_usb4_host *host,
+					   struct usb_role_switch *sw)
+{
+	int ret;
+
+	if (!sw)
+		return;
+	ret = usb_role_switch_unhold(sw);
+	if (ret)
+		dev_warn(host->dev, "%s: USB role not set after the hand-back: %d\n",
+			 dev_name(host->tunnel_ctrl), ret);
+	usb_role_switch_put(sw);
+}
+
 static void qcom_usb4_host_release(struct qcom_usb4_host *host)
 {
+	struct usb_role_switch *sw = NULL;
+
 	dev_dbg(host->dev, "%s: stopping USB4 host router %u\n",
 		host->suspending ? "system sleep" : "no USB4 partner", host->port);
+	/*
+	 * Not for system sleep: the partner has not left USB4 then, and the
+	 * router is started again at resume. The hold must not be taken then
+	 * either: it waits for the controller's role work, which is on a
+	 * freezable workqueue.
+	 */
+	if (!host->suspending)
+		sw = qcom_usb4_host_usb_host_leave(host);
 	qcom_usb4_host_stop_all(host);
 	host->released = true;
+	qcom_usb4_host_usb_host_return(host, sw);
 }
 
 static int qcom_usb4_host_acquire(struct qcom_usb4_host *host)
-- 
2.56.1

